<<setup, include=FALSE, purl=TRUE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo = TRUE, eval = FALSE, purl = FALSE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE,
  comment = "#>", tidy = FALSE, size = "small"
)
options(width = 92)
library(CoTiMA2)
@

\begin{abstract}
CoTiMA2 ordinarily delegates continuous-time model estimation to ctsem and its
compiled Stan implementation.  CoTiMA2's optional Julia backend evaluates the
same ctsem model and data representation through Julia and offers maximum
likelihood estimation and Bayesian NUTS sampling.  Its purpose is to provide a
potentially faster route for models in which repeated subject-level likelihood
and gradient calculations dominate computation, while retaining ctsem's model
parameterization and transformations.  This document explains why the backend
exists, how it fits into the usual CoTiMA2 workflow, how fitted and unfitted
CoTiMA2 objects differ, and how to obtain estimates, uncertainty, transformed
ctsem summaries, and study-specific results.  The Julia study-level
random-effects route is introduced only briefly because it is documented in
the separate CoTiMA-RE guide.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Why add Julia to CoTiMA2?}

CoTiMA2 combines participant-level longitudinal data from several primary
studies and constructs continuous-time structural equation models through
ctsem.  These models can be computationally demanding.  The same likelihood
must be evaluated for many persons, observation intervals, and parameter
values during optimization or Bayesian sampling.  Models with many
time-independent predictor (TI-predictor) effects---including study indicators
or weighted-effect-coded study effects---can be especially expensive.

The Julia backend was added for three related reasons:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Subject-level parallelism.} Contributions from different
    subjects can be evaluated concurrently using Julia threads.
  \item \textbf{Fast automatic differentiation.} Subject-specific gradients
    and Hessians can be calculated without repeatedly passing through R.
  \item \textbf{An additional estimation engine.} The same prepared ctsem
    model can be estimated by Julia L-BFGS for maximum likelihood or by
    AdvancedHMC.jl's NUTS implementation for Bayesian estimation.
\end{enumerate}

Julia is therefore an \emph{alternative computational backend}, not a new
statistical model.  The model matrices, raw parameterization, parameter
transformations, data layout, TI-predictor effects, and ctsem prior definitions
remain those generated by ctsem.  Numerical agreement between the Julia and
Stan likelihoods should be checked whenever a new model branch is used.

\important{The Julia backend is currently experimental.  It can be much faster
for some models, but it is not guaranteed to outperform ctsem's compiled Stan
code.  Performance depends on sample size, number of time points, number and
kind of parameters, TI-predictor structure, matrix dimensions, number of
threads, optimizer geometry, and---for Bayesian estimation---the number of NUTS
leapfrog steps.  Always begin with a small validation or timing run.}

\subsection{What remains unchanged?}

ctsem uses unconstrained ``raw'' parameters internally and transforms them to
valid substantive parameters.  Examples include positive variance parameters
and stability-preserving transformations of diagonal drift parameters.  The
Julia likelihood uses the raw parameters and the transformation instructions
contained in the ctsem setup.  Consequently, the reported DRIFT, DIFFUSION,
T0VAR, and other matrices have the same interpretation as in ctsem.

Small numerical differences are normal because optimizers and linear-algebra
libraries need not follow identical paths.  A large log-likelihood difference
at the same raw parameter vector, however, signals an incompatibility and must
not be dismissed as an optimizer difference.

\section{Software components}

The current development version uses two principal files:
\begin{description}
  \item[\code{ctmaJuliaFit.R}] The R-facing interface.  It recognizes CoTiMA2 or
    ctsem objects, creates a ctsem setup when necessary, starts Julia through
    JuliaCall, validates and controls estimation, and converts results into an
    R object with familiar \code{print()}, \code{summary()}, \code{coef()}, and
    \code{vcov()} methods.
  \item[\code{CtsemJulia.jl}] The Julia implementation of the ctsem likelihood,
    derivatives, optimization, Hessian calculations, and AdvancedHMC NUTS
    interface.  Keeping the numerical implementation in Julia is necessary:
    translating every likelihood and gradient evaluation back through R would
    remove much of the performance benefit.
\end{description}

During package development, \code{julia\_file} identifies the local
\code{CtsemJulia.jl}.  In an installed CoTiMA2 package, the file resides
under \code{inst/julia/CtsemJulia.jl}; \code{system.file()} can then find it and
ordinary users should not need to supply \code{julia\_file}.

The Julia \code{Project.toml} and \code{Manifest.toml} describe the Julia
packages used by the backend.  They make the computational environment more
reproducible.  They are implementation files rather than model input files.

\section{Loading the user-guide examples explicitly}

CoTiMA2 does not attach the user-guide datasets automatically.  This avoids a
dangerous ambiguity: a request such as \code{selectedStudies=1:10} must not
silently use packaged \code{delta\_t1}, \code{sampleSize1}, or
\code{empcov1} objects when the user has not created study 1.  Load only the
examples required for an exercise:

<<load-examples, purl=FALSE>>=
ctmaLoadExamples(c(
  "delta_t1", "sampleSize1", "empcov1",
  "delta_t4", "sampleSize4", "empcov4"
))

exampleStudies <- ctmaPrep(selectedStudies = c(1, 4))
@

With \code{names=NULL}, \code{ctmaLoadExamples()} loads every packaged guide
object.  Existing objects are not overwritten unless
\code{overwrite=TRUE}.  After an exercise, use:

<<unload-examples, purl=FALSE>>=
ctmaUnloadExamples()
@

Only objects that remain byte-for-byte identical to the examples originally
loaded by the function are removed.  Modified objects are retained and named
in a message, so user work cannot be deleted inadvertently.  Both functions
accept an \code{envir} argument when a dedicated environment is preferable to
the global environment.

\section{The current CoTiMA2 workflow}

The workflow begins with CoTiMA2 data preparation and model specification.
The estimation backend can now be selected independently when fitting the
primary studies with \code{ctmaInit()} and when fitting the pooled model with
\code{ctmaFit()}.  Thus Julia is no longer restricted to the pooled-model
stage.  It changes the numerical estimation engine, not the substantive model
or data-preparation steps.

\subsection{Three backend names}

\begin{longtable}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.16\linewidth}
                  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.27\linewidth}
                  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.48\linewidth}}
\toprule
Backend & Implementation & Current role\\
\midrule
\code{"stan"} & Regular ctsem/Stan backend & Default route for established
  ctsem maximum-likelihood and Bayesian workflows.\\
\code{"julia"} & CoTiMA2's own Julia backend & Available directly in
  \code{ctmaInit()} and \code{ctmaFit()}; supports ML and Bayesian NUTS and
  exposes the low-level \code{ctmaJuliaFit()} interface.\\
\code{"ctsemjulia"} & Charles Driver's experimental native ctsem Julia
  backend & Available in \code{ctmaInit()} and \code{ctmaFit()} for ML and
  Bayesian NUTS or SAEM; ordinary models use the direct route, while
  random-effects models use Laplace integration or joint SAEM. It requires a
  compatible experimental ctsem version.\\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

The two Julia names are not interchangeable.  In a direct experimental
\code{ctsem::ctFit()} call, Charles Driver's backend is named
\code{backend="julia"}; from CoTiMA2 it is deliberately named
\code{backend="ctsemjulia"} to distinguish it from CoTiMA2's own Julia
implementation.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
