\subsection[Step 1: fit the primary studies with ctmaInit()]{Step 1: fit the primary studies with \code{ctmaInit()}}

\code{ctmaInit()} fits the primary studies separately.  This step checks data
quality and model plausibility and supplies information used to construct the
pooled model.  CoTiMA2's Julia backend can perform these primary-study fits
directly.  With \code{optimize=TRUE} (the default) it uses ML; with
\code{optimize=FALSE} it uses Bayesian NUTS.  The exact arguments depend on the
substantive model; a typical Julia ML call is:

<<ctma-init, purl=FALSE>>=
initFit <- ctmaInit(
  primaryStudies = primaryStudies,
  activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
  n.latent = 2,
  backend = "julia",
  coresToUse = -1,
  doPar = 1
)
@

Primary studies are currently processed sequentially with either Julia
backend, so \code{doPar} must equal 1.  Within each study,
\code{coresToUse} is translated into a Julia thread allocation unless
\code{julia\_threads} or \code{subject\_threads} is supplied explicitly in
\code{backendArgs}.  ML primary-study fits calculate Hessian uncertainty and
ctsem-transformed results because \code{ctmaInit()} requires study-specific
standard errors and summaries.  Its \code{finishsamples} argument supplies
the default number of transformed draws; an explicit
\code{backendArgs$transformed\_draws} takes precedence.

For example, the controls can be made explicit as follows:

<<ctma-init-julia-controls, purl=FALSE>>=
initFit <- ctmaInit(
  primaryStudies = primaryStudies,
  activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
  n.latent = 2,
  backend = "julia",
  coresToUse = -1,
  doPar = 1,
  backendArgs = list(
    julia_threads = "auto",
    subject_threads = "auto",
    blas_threads = 1L,
    uncertainty = TRUE,
    transformed = TRUE
  )
)
@

The last two controls are shown for clarity; they are already true for an ML
\code{ctmaInit()} fit.  Setting \code{optimize=FALSE} requests Bayesian
estimation and activates the corresponding chain, warmup, and sampling
controls returned by \code{ctmaBackendDefaults()}.

Inspect warnings, convergence, study-specific drift estimates, and implausible
standard errors before pooling.  A fast pooled backend cannot repair a poorly
identified primary-study model.

\subsection[Step 2: construct the pooled model with ctmaFit()]{Step 2: construct the pooled model with \code{ctmaFit()}}

The pooled CoTiMA2 model can be estimated directly with the selected backend.
For most users, this high-level interface is preferable to calling
\code{ctmaJuliaFit()} manually.

<<ctma-fit-branches, purl=FALSE>>=
# Option A: estimate directly with CoTiMA2's Julia backend
pooledJuliaFit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "julia",
  coresToUse = -1
)

# Option B: estimate through the ordinary ctsem/Stan route
pooledStanFit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  fit = TRUE,
  coresToUse = 4
)

# Option C: create only the pooled model and data for advanced low-level work
pooledSetup <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  fit = FALSE
)

# Inspect or save the executable model specification
cat(pooledSetup$ctModelText)

# Reconstruct the generated ctsem model as an object named ctModel
eval(parse(text = pooledSetup$ctModelText))
@

\subsubsection{Recommended high-level Julia route}

\code{ctmaFit(backend="julia")} constructs the pooled setup, invokes
\code{ctmaJuliaFit()} internally, and returns the completed Julia fit.  Model
arguments remain top-level \code{ctmaFit()} arguments; numerical controls
belong in \code{backendArgs}.  This is the ordinary user interface.

\subsubsection[Advanced fitted and unfitted routes]{Advanced \code{fit=TRUE} and \code{fit=FALSE} routes}

\begin{itemize}
  \item ctsem/Stan performs the pooled estimation before Julia is called.
  \item The fitted raw estimates provide strong Julia initial values.
  \item \code{ctmaJuliaFit(validate=TRUE)} can compare the Julia and Stan
    likelihoods at exactly the same raw parameter vector.
  \item The existing ctsem covariance and summary offer a direct benchmark.
  \item The disadvantage is computational: if the Stan fit takes days, it
    defeats the purpose of using Julia as the first estimator.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection[Implications of fit = FALSE]{Implications of \code{fit = FALSE}}

\begin{itemize}
  \item CoTiMA2 returns the original data and ctsem base model without fitting
    the pooled model. It additionally returns \code{ctModelText}, an executable
    textual representation of the final model specification.
  \item The generated model can be inspected with
    \code{cat(result\$ctModelText)} and reconstructed with
    \code{eval(parse(text = result\$ctModelText))}. The reconstructed object is
    named \code{ctModel}; it includes CoTiMA2's final parameter table and
    post-construction constraints.
  \item \code{ctmaJuliaFit()} internally asks ctsem to generate the required
    setup data and code; Stan optimization or sampling is not performed.
  \item With no supplied \code{inits}, the initial raw vector is zero.  Zero is
    valid computationally but may be inefficient for a difficult model.
  \item A user-supplied raw vector can be passed through \code{inits}.  It must
    match the raw parameter ordering and length of the generated ctsem setup.
  \item A Stan likelihood benchmark is normally unavailable because no Stan
    fit exists.  Compatibility checks still run, but validation against Stan
    should first be established on a smaller representative model.
  \item Transformed summaries need a ctsem setup.  They may be requested during
    fitting or added later with \code{ctAddJuliaSamples()} using the matching
    fitted or \code{fit=FALSE} CoTiMA2 object.
\end{itemize}

Use \code{fit=FALSE} when a reusable setup or explicit low-level
\code{ctmaJuliaFit()} call is required.  Use a completed Stan fit during
backend validation, when retained starting values are important, or when the
ordinary fit is already available.  Otherwise prefer the direct
\code{ctmaFit(backend="julia")} route.

\section{Binary manifest variables}

CoTiMA2 supports models containing continuous and binary manifest variables
with either the Stan or Julia backend.  Binary observations must be coded as
zero and one.  The \code{binaries} argument contains one entry for every
manifest variable: use 1 for a binary variable and 0 for a continuous
variable, in the same order as the manifest-variable names.

For example, the following specification treats the first manifest variable
as binary and the second as continuous.  The same binary specification should
be used while fitting the primary studies and while constructing the pooled
model.

<<binary-cint-example, purl=FALSE>>=
initBinary <- ctmaInit(
  primaryStudies = primaryStudies,
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  cint = "auto",
  backend = "julia"
)

pooledBinary <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initBinary,
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  cint = "auto",
  manifestMeans = 0,
  backend = "julia"
)
@

\subsection{Why a location parameter is required}

For a binary manifest variable, the linear measurement predictor is converted
to a response probability through the inverse-logit function.  A predictor of
zero therefore implies a probability of 0.5.  If both the relevant continuous
time intercept (CINT) and the corresponding manifest mean are fixed to zero,
the response location is fixed by the model instead of estimated from the
data.  This can force an implausible long-run event probability and can produce
a misspecified model even when the remaining dynamic parameters are free.

Every binary manifest variable must consequently have at least one estimated
location component:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a freely estimated CINT connected to that manifest variable through
    the LAMBDA loading matrix; or
  \item a freely estimated MANIFESTMEANS parameter for that manifest variable.
\end{itemize}

The CINT parameterization places the location in the latent continuous-time
process.  This is usually preferable when the scientific interpretation is a
long-run latent-process level.  The MANIFESTMEANS parameterization places the
location directly in the measurement equation and may be preferable when the
binary response requires its own measurement intercept.  Normally one of
these alternatives should be free and the other fixed: freeing both can make
the two location components difficult or impossible to identify separately.

The equivalent measurement-intercept specification is:

<<binary-manifest-mean-example, purl=FALSE>>=
pooledBinary <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initBinary,
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  cint = 0,
  manifestMeans = "auto",
  backend = "julia"
)
@

CoTiMA2 checks the final ctsem parameter table before fitting.  It stops when a
binary variable has neither a free connected CINT nor a free MANIFESTMEANS
parameter.  It also checks that explicit CINT and manifest-mean vectors match
the latent and manifest dimensions, respectively.  Models using
\code{indVarying="CINT"}, \code{indVarying="MANIFEST"},
\code{randomIntercepts="CINT"}, or
\code{randomIntercepts="MANIFEST"} are exempt from this check because the
requested location component is already estimated by that specification.

For \code{backend="julia"}, the restricted specifications
\code{indVarying="CINT"} and \code{indVarying="MANIFEST"} are translated
automatically into Julia-compatible time-invariant phantom processes.  Their
baseline random-intercept (co-)variance matrix remains freely estimated, but
TI effects on phantom variances and on covariances between substantive T0
states and phantom states are fixed to zero.  Consequently, one common
person-level random-intercept covariance matrix is estimated across studies.
The phantom means can still be estimated and influenced by TI predictors.
This is different from setting the phantom variance itself to zero, which
would remove the person-level random effect.

The pooled construction deliberately separates person-level random intercepts
from later study-level random effects.  A future study-random-effects or
Laplace layer can therefore place a distribution across studies on selected
parameters without reintroducing ctsem's unsupported state-dependent
\code{nindvarying} branch.

The Julia backend evaluates binary observations with a logit response model,
adds their Bernoulli log-likelihood contributions, and uses the corresponding
ctsem measurement Jacobian and Bernoulli variance in the extended Kalman
update.  Mixed continuous/binary models are supported; ordinal manifest types
other than binary zero/one responses are not yet supported.

\section{Selecting backend controls}

Model-defining arguments remain ordinary \code{ctmaInit()} or
\code{ctmaFit()} arguments.  Backend-specific numerical controls belong in a
named \code{backendArgs} list.  Inspect the applicable defaults explicitly:

<<backend-defaults, purl=FALSE>>=
initJuliaDefaults <- ctmaBackendDefaults(
  backend = "julia", estimation = "ML", workflow = "ctmaInit"
)

fitJuliaDefaults <- ctmaBackendDefaults(
  backend = "julia", estimation = "ML", workflow = "ctmaFit"
)

fitCtsemJuliaDefaults <- ctmaBackendDefaults(
  backend = "ctsemjulia", estimation = "ML", workflow = "ctmaFit"
)
@

Calling \code{ctmaBackendDefaults()} without arguments returns only the Stan
defaults; it does not combine defaults from all backends.  CoTiMA2's Julia ML
defaults expose \code{uncertainty=TRUE}.  The native ctsem Julia defaults
expose \code{optimcontrol$uncertainty="hessian"}.  Both therefore request
uncertainty for regular ML fits, although the route and returned fit objects
differ.  Expensive post-fit calculations can be omitted deliberately during
development diagnostics, but reported inference should be based on a
converged fit with usable curvature.

\section[Advanced fitting with ctmaJuliaFit()]{Advanced fitting with \code{ctmaJuliaFit()}}

Ordinary analyses should select \code{backend="julia"} in
\code{ctmaInit()} or \code{ctmaFit()}.  The exported
\code{ctmaJuliaFit()} function remains useful as a lower-level interface for
same-vector likelihood validation, setup-only objects, custom Hessian
recalculation, and advanced diagnostics.

\subsection{Maximum likelihood}

The following call shows the principal controls.  Comments after arguments are
brief reminders; the complete explanations follow.

<<julia-ml, purl=FALSE>>=
mlFit <- ctmaJuliaFit(
  object = pooledSetup,               # fitted object, fit=FALSE object, or RDS path
  initialize = TRUE,                  # start JuliaCall if necessary
  estimation = "ML",                 # maximum-likelihood estimation
  inits = NULL,                       # retained estimates, or zero for fit=FALSE
  carefulFit = TRUE,                  # staged optimization for TI effects
  multistart = 4,                     # one exact plus three jittered starts
  parallel_starts = 4,                # run the starts concurrently
  threads_per_start = 2,              # subject threads in each ML process
  multistart_jitter = 0.10,           # relative jitter after the first start
  warm_start = "auto",               # guarded subset pilot for starting values
  warm_start_fraction = 0.10,        # initial requested fraction of persons
  warm_start_maxiters = 250,         # iterations of the subset pilot
  maxiters = 5000,                    # maximum iterations per stage
  gradient_tolerance = 0.005,         # raw-gradient convergence requirement
  uncertainty = TRUE,                 # observed Hessian and covariance
  transformed = FALSE,                # transformed summary may be added later
  julia_threads = 6,                  # subject-level Julia threads
  blas_threads = 1,                   # avoid nested BLAS parallelism
  show_trace = TRUE,
  seed = 123
)
@

\subsection{Bayesian NUTS estimation}

<<julia-bayes, purl=FALSE>>=
bayesFit <- ctmaJuliaFit(
  object = pooledSetup,
  estimation = "Bayes",
  inits = coef(mlFit, type = "raw"),  # a converged ML point is often useful
  chains = 2,
  warmup = 500,
  samples = 500,
  target_accept = 0.90,
  max_depth = 8,
  metric = "dense",
  init_jitter = 0.01,
  nuts_progress = TRUE,
  parallel_chains = 2,                # run both chains concurrently
  threads_per_chain = 4,              # subject threads in each chain process
  julia_threads = 6,
  blas_threads = 1,
  seed = 123
)
@

Bayesian chains can run concurrently in separate local Julia processes; within
each chain, independent subject likelihood contributions use the requested
threads.  A dense
metric can represent posterior correlations but needs substantially more
warmup than a diagonal metric, especially when there are many parameters.

\subsection[Arguments to ctmaJuliaFit()]{Arguments to \code{ctmaJuliaFit()}}

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{object}] A fitted ctsem object, fitted CoTiMA2 object, unfitted
    \code{ctmaFit(fit=FALSE)} object, or path to an RDS file containing one.
  \item[\code{study}] Selects an element when \code{studyFitList} contains
    several fits.  It is otherwise ignored.
  \item[\code{julia\_file}] Optional development override for the path to
    \code{CtsemJulia.jl}.  Installed-package users should leave it at
    \code{NULL}; CoTiMA2 locates its packaged source automatically.
  \item[\code{initialize}] If \code{TRUE}, initialize JuliaCall when Julia is
    not active.  Julia cannot be restarted with a different thread count in
    the same R process.
  \item[\code{julia\_home}] Directory used by JuliaCall to locate Julia.  The
    default automatically searches the system path and common Juliaup
    installation locations; supply it only for an unusual installation.
  \item[\code{validate}] Compare Julia's likelihood with the retained Stan
    likelihood when the latter is available.
  \item[\code{tolerance}] Maximum accepted absolute Julia--Stan likelihood
    difference during validation.
  \item[\code{optimize}] Older logical shortcut.  \code{TRUE} selects ML;
    new code should preferably use \code{estimation="ML"}.
  \item[\code{estimation}] \code{"none"} evaluates/validates without fitting,
    \code{"ML"} runs L-BFGS, and \code{"Bayes"} runs NUTS.  MAP is not
    currently exposed by \code{ctmaJuliaFit()}.
  \item[\code{uncertainty}] For ML, calculate the observed Hessian, its inverse
    covariance, standard errors, and normal-theory raw intervals after
    convergence.  This can be expensive and is skipped if the raw-gradient
    convergence criterion is not met.
  \item[\code{inits}] Optional raw-scale starting vector.  A fitted object's
    retained raw estimates are the default; an unfitted object otherwise uses
    a correctly sized zero vector.
  \item[\code{maxiters}] Maximum number of L-BFGS iterations in each active
    optimization stage.  It is a ceiling, not a required iteration count.
  \item[\code{chunksize}] ForwardDiff chunk size for derivatives.  The default
    is generally suitable; changing it is an advanced performance adjustment.
  \item[\code{gradient\_tolerance}] Maximum absolute raw-scale gradient required
    to regard an ML fit as converged.
  \item[\code{function\_relative\_tolerance}] Optional relative objective-change
    stopping rule.  Zero disables it so a small objective change cannot hide a
    large raw gradient.
  \item[\code{parameter\_relative\_tolerance}] Optional relative parameter-change
    stopping rule.  Zero disables it.
  \item[\code{optimizer\_initial\_step}] Controls the target largest raw-coordinate
    displacement in the automatically preconditioned first L-BFGS step.
  \item[\code{multistart}] Number of independent ML optimizations.  Start one
    uses \code{inits} unchanged; remaining starts are reproducibly jittered.
  \item[\code{parallel\_starts}] Number of ML starts run concurrently in
    separate local Julia processes.
  \item[\code{threads\_per\_start}] Subject-likelihood threads allocated to each
    ML process.  Budget \code{parallel\_starts * threads\_per\_start} cores.
  \item[\code{multistart\_jitter}] Relative raw-scale perturbation for starts
    after the first.  The default 0.1 is a ten-percent perturbation, with a
    small scale floor for parameters initialized at zero.
  \item[\code{warm\_start}] \code{"auto"} (the default), \code{TRUE}, or
    \code{FALSE}. Before the ordinary full-data ML fit, the automatic option
    may estimate population/base parameters on a stratified subset of complete
    persons while keeping TI-predictor effects fixed at zero. Explicitly
    supplied \code{inits} disable this pilot.
  \item[\code{warm\_start\_fraction}] Initially requested fraction of persons
    (default 0.10). This is not a hard limit: identification, study, repeated-
    measurement, and binary-category checks can enlarge the pilot.
  \item[\code{warm\_start\_subjects\_per\_parameter}] Minimum number of informative
    persons per population/base parameter (default 20).
  \item[\code{warm\_start\_minimum\_per\_study}] Minimum number of persons sampled
    from every study (default 25), or every available person in a smaller
    study.
  \item[\code{warm\_start\_maximum\_subjects}] Soft computational ceiling
    (default 5000). Structural minima take priority over this ceiling.
  \item[\code{warm\_start\_binary\_minimum}] Minimum persons representing every
    available zero and one category of each binary manifest (default 20).
  \item[\code{warm\_start\_maxiters}] Maximum L-BFGS iterations in the subset
    pilot (default 250).
  \item[\code{carefulFit}] If \code{TRUE} (recommended), first optimize
    population parameters with TI effects fixed, then include TI effects, and
    finally perform a jointly rescaled refinement when needed. When complete
    raw estimates from a previous fit of the identical model are supplied via
    \code{inits}, setting \code{carefulFit=FALSE} usually provides the more
    efficient refinement because all parameters are optimized jointly from
    that retained point.
  \item[\code{staged\_optimization}] Deprecated alias for \code{carefulFit}; do
    not use it in new analyses.
  \item[\code{confidence}] Confidence level used for ML raw-parameter intervals.
  \item[\code{transformed}] Transform approximate raw draws through ctsem and
    construct ctsem-style matrices and summaries.  For a large ML fit it is
    often convenient to set this to \code{FALSE} and add samples later.
  \item[\code{transformed\_draws}] Number of raw draws used to approximate
    nonlinear transformed summaries.
  \item[\code{transformed\_cores}] Cores used by ctsem while transforming draws.
    These are distinct from Julia likelihood threads.
  \item[\code{seed}] Random seed for transformed draws and NUTS sampling.
  \item[\code{chains}] Number of independent NUTS chains.
  \item[\code{warmup}] Adaptation iterations per NUTS chain.  Warmup draws are
    not retained for inference.
  \item[\code{samples}] Retained posterior draws per chain.
  \item[\code{target\_accept}] Target NUTS acceptance probability.  Values near
    0.8--0.9 are common; increasing it may reduce divergences at the cost of
    smaller steps and more computation.
  \item[\code{max\_depth}] Maximum NUTS tree depth.  Repeated retained-sample
    depth hits indicate truncated trajectories; increasing it also raises the
    maximum possible computational work per iteration.
  \item[\code{metric}] \code{"diagonal"} adapts one scale per parameter;
    \code{"dense"} also adapts correlations and therefore requires more
    warmup and memory.
  \item[\code{init\_jitter}] Standard deviation of chain-specific perturbations
    around \code{inits} on the raw scale.
  \item[\code{nuts\_progress}] Show compact aggregate NUTS progress across all
    chains, including completed iterations, elapsed time, and an ETA. The line
    is overwritten at each update where supported by the console.
  \item[\code{progress\_every}] Number of NUTS iterations between progress
    messages from each parallel worker (default 25). Smaller values update the
    display more often but add a little inter-process communication.
  \item[\code{parallel\_chains}] Number of Bayesian chains run concurrently in
    separate local Julia worker processes.  One retains sequential behavior.
  \item[\code{threads\_per\_chain}] Subject-likelihood threads assigned to every
    concurrent chain.  Ensure that \code{parallel\_chains * threads\_per\_chain}
    does not exceed the intended CPU budget.
  \item[\code{performance\_warning}] Print a heuristic warning when ctsem's
    compiled Stan route may be faster for the model at hand.
  \item[\code{show\_trace}] Print the ML optimizer trace.  The latest trace line
    overwrites the preceding line where the console supports carriage returns.
  \item[\code{julia\_threads}] Julia threads used for subject likelihoods and
    gradients.  The value is fixed when Julia starts; restart R before changing
    it in an already initialized session.
  \item[\code{blas\_threads}] Threads used by BLAS inside each calculation.
    \code{1} is normally appropriate because ctsem matrices are small and
    subject-level Julia parallelism is already active.
\end{description}

\section{Inspecting and extending a Julia fit}

\subsection{Printing and extracting results}

<<inspect, purl=FALSE>>=
print(mlFit)
coef(mlFit, type = "raw")
vcov(mlFit)

# Available after transformed samples have been created
coef(mlFit, type = "population")
coef(mlFit, type = "tipred")
coef(mlFit, type = "matrices")
coef(mlFit, type = "study")
summary(mlFit, digits = 4, study_specific = TRUE)
@

The user-facing methods are:
\begin{description}
  \item[\code{print(x)}] Gives a compact account of the backend, estimation
    method, likelihood, timing, convergence, curvature, and sampler diagnostics.
  \item[\code{coef(object, type, ...)}] \code{type="raw"} returns the raw
    parameter vector.  Other choices are \code{"population"}, \code{"tipred"},
    \code{"matrices"}, \code{"study"}, and \code{"transformed"}; they require
    transformed samples.
  \item[\code{vcov(object, ...)}] Returns the inverse-Hessian covariance for
    ML or the empirical covariance of retained raw NUTS draws for Bayes.
  \item[\code{summary(object, ctsem\_fit, transformed\_draws,
    transformed\_cores, seed, digits, study\_specific, ...)}] Returns the stored
    ctsem-style summary or creates it using a matching setup.  \code{digits}
    controls printed decimals.  If \code{study\_specific=TRUE}, study-specific
    estimates evaluated at each observed TI-predictor profile are appended;
    this supports dummy and weighted-effect coding.
\end{description}

\subsection{Calculating uncertainty after a fast point-estimate fit}

For a large model, it can be useful to separate optimization from uncertainty
calculation.  Setting \code{uncertainty=FALSE} and
\code{transformed=FALSE} returns the ML point estimates without calculating
the subject-wise observed Hessian or transforming approximate draws.  Save the
result because it contains both the fitted raw estimates and a reference to the
source setup:

<<save-point-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
saveRDS(pointFit, "pointFit_without_uncertainty.rds")
@

Before requesting uncertainty, verify that optimization met the raw-gradient
criterion.  Standard errors, confidence intervals, and p-values should not be
interpreted at a point that has not converged adequately.

<<check-point-convergence, purl=FALSE>>=
pointFit$optimization$converged
pointFit$optimization$maximum_absolute_gradient
pointFit$optimization$stage_iterations
pointFit$optimization$stage_maximum_absolute_gradients
@

If convergence is adequate, run \code{ctmaJuliaFit()} with
\code{estimation="none"} and \code{optimize=FALSE}.  Supplying the retained
raw coefficients through \code{inits} makes the Hessian calculation start
directly at the completed optimum; L-BFGS is not run again.  The source object
retained by an ordinary \code{ctmaFit(backend="julia")} call can normally be
used as the matching setup.  A separately retained
\code{ctmaFit(fit=FALSE)} setup describing the same model is equally suitable.

<<add-uncertainty-later, purl=FALSE>>=
pointFit <- readRDS("pointFit_without_uncertainty.rds")

uncertaintyFit <- ctmaJuliaFit(
  object = pointFit$source_object,
  estimation = "none",
  optimize = FALSE,
  inits = coef(pointFit, type = "raw"),
  uncertainty = TRUE,
  transformed = FALSE,
  confidence = 0.95,
  chunksize = 7,
  julia_threads = 8,
  blas_threads = 1
)
@

The raw-scale table contains estimates, standard errors, confidence limits,
and normal-theory z statistics.  Two-sided normal-theory p-values can be added
as follows:

<<raw-uncertainty-results, purl=FALSE>>=
rawResults <- uncertaintyFit$information$raw_summary
rawResults$p_value <- 2 * pnorm(-abs(rawResults$z))
rawResults

vcov(uncertaintyFit)
@

These p-values concern the raw ctsem coordinates and use the local
normal/Hessian approximation.  They are not Bayesian posterior probabilities.
For nonlinear transformed parameters, use the transformed summaries and
intervals rather than mechanically interpreting a raw-coordinate p-value.

When the source is a lightweight \code{fit=FALSE} setup, transformed summaries
are added in a second step.  This uses the covariance matrix just calculated
and still does not repeat optimization:

<<transform-after-uncertainty, purl=FALSE>>=
uncertaintyFit <- ctAddJuliaSamples(
  fit = uncertaintyFit,
  setup = pointFit$source_object,
  nsamples = 1000,
  cores = 2,
  seed = 123,
  digits = 4
)

summary(uncertaintyFit, digits = 4, study_specific = TRUE)
coef(uncertaintyFit, type = "population")
coef(uncertaintyFit, type = "matrices")
coef(uncertaintyFit, type = "study")
@

The Julia thread count is fixed when Julia starts.  If a different value is
used for the later Hessian calculation, restart R before calling
\code{ctmaJuliaFit()} and use the intended \code{julia\_threads} value in the
first Julia call of that session.

\subsection{Adding transformed samples later}

There is no need to repeat a costly Julia optimization merely because
\code{transformed=FALSE} was used.  \code{ctAddJuliaSamples()} plays a role
similar to \code{ctsem::ctAddSamples()}.  For an ML fit it requires a stored
Hessian covariance matrix, either calculated during the original fit or added
later as described above:

<<add-samples, purl=FALSE>>=
mlFit <- ctAddJuliaSamples(
  fit = mlFit,                # completed Julia ML or Bayesian fit
  setup = pooledSetup,        # matching fitted or fit=FALSE CoTiMA2 object
  nsamples = 1000,
  cores = 2,
  seed = 123,
  digits = 4
)

summary(mlFit, digits = 4, study_specific = TRUE)
studyEstimates <- coef(mlFit, type = "study")
@

Its arguments are:
\begin{description}
  \item[\code{fit}] Completed \code{ctmaJuliaFit} object.
  \item[\code{setup}] Matching fitted or unfitted CoTiMA2/ctsem setup.  It must
    describe exactly the same raw parameterization.
  \item[\code{nsamples}] Number of transformed draws.  ML draws come from the
    Hessian-normal approximation.  Bayesian input uses at most this many actual
    retained NUTS draws.
  \item[\code{cores}] Cores passed to ctsem's transformation routine.
  \item[\code{seed}] Optional reproducibility seed.
  \item[\code{digits}] Decimal places used in the constructed summary.
\end{description}

The backward-compatible name \code{ctsmAddJuliaSamples()} currently aliases
\code{ctAddJuliaSamples()}, but new code should use the latter.

\section{Choosing ML, MAP, or Bayesian estimation}

\begin{longtable}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.15\linewidth}
                  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.27\linewidth}
                  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.49\linewidth}}
\toprule
Estimator & Current Julia access & Interpretation and practical use\\
\midrule
ML & \code{ctmaJuliaFit(estimation="ML")} & Maximizes the likelihood without
  priors.  It is useful for point estimation and likelihood-based model
  comparisons.  Hessian uncertainty requires adequate convergence and local
  positive curvature.\\
MAP & Not currently exposed by \code{ctmaJuliaFit()}; available through the
  Stan backend of \code{ctmaReFit()} & Maximizes likelihood plus log prior.
  MAP is a posterior mode, not a set of posterior draws, and optimizer-based
  intervals are not currently supported for the study-RE fit.\\
Bayes & \code{ctmaJuliaFit(estimation="Bayes")} and the restricted Julia
  route in \code{ctmaReFit()} & Uses AdvancedHMC NUTS and the ctsem-defined
  priors.  Retained draws represent posterior uncertainty.  Multiple chains,
  sufficient warmup, and diagnostic inspection are required.\\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\section{Julia estimation for study-level random effects}

The separate CoTiMA-RE methodology and usage document explains random-effect
selection, transformations, covariance structures, and interpretation.  In
brief, prepare the hierarchical model with \code{ctmaRePrep()} and select an
estimator and backend with \code{ctmaReFit()}:

<<re-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
preparedRE <- ctmaRePrep(
  model = mlFit,
  reDRIFT = reDRIFT,
  covariance = "independent",
  parameterization = "noncentered",
  init = "auto"
)

# Bayesian Stan route
reStan <- ctmaReFit(
  prepared = preparedRE,
  estimation = "bayes",
  backend = "stan",
  chains = 4,
  iter = 2000
)

# Bayesian Julia route (current supported subset)
reJulia <- ctmaReFit(
  prepared = preparedRE,
  estimation = "bayes",
  backend = "julia",
  chains = 2,
  init_jitter = 0.01,
  backendArgs = list(
    julia_threads = 6,
    blas_threads = 1,
    warmup = 500,
    samples = 500,
    target_accept = 0.9,
    max_depth = 8,
    metric = "dense",
    parallel_chains = 2,
    threads_per_chain = 4
  )
)

# MAP or conditional/joint ML currently use Stan
reMAP <- ctmaReFit(preparedRE, estimation = "map", backend = "stan")
reML  <- ctmaReFit(preparedRE, estimation = "ml",  backend = "stan")
@

At present the Julia study-RE backend supports Bayesian estimation for
independent, noncentered, type-1 raw/DRIFT study effects.  Stan remains the
general backend for MAP, conditional/joint ML, correlated random effects, and
other random-effect types.  The \code{backendArgs} list contains Julia-specific
overrides; the top-level \code{chains}, \code{seed}, and \code{init\_jitter}
remain ordinary \code{ctmaReFit()} arguments.

\subsection{Experimental native ctsem Julia route}

CoTiMA2 also provides an experimental, feature-gated route to ctsem's native
multilevel Julia implementation.  It is selected with
\code{backend="ctsemjulia"}.  This is distinct from both CoTiMA2's own
\code{backend="julia"} and the \code{ctmaRePrep()}--\code{ctmaReFit()}
workflow described above. Without random effects, it performs ordinary native
ctsem Julia estimation and lets \code{intoverpop="auto"} resolve to the direct
path. With \code{optimize=TRUE}, subject- or study-level Gaussian random
effects use Laplace ML. With \code{optimize=FALSE}, the native ctsem sampler
can use native automatic sampling, or an explicit NUTS, SAEM, learned-map
NUTS, or tempered SMC kernel. The integration target is chosen separately.

Every CoTiMA2 function that exposes a \code{backend} argument checks an
explicit \code{backend="ctsemjulia"} request before doing substantive work.
If the required native API or \code{JuliaConnectoR} is missing, the resulting
error reports the installed ctsem version and missing capabilities.  It also
prints an ``at your own risk'' installation recipe for Charles Driver's
\code{juliaFit} branch.  That recipe deliberately uses a separate R library;
it does not overwrite the production ctsem installation.  Restart R and put
the isolated library first in \code{.libPaths()} before loading CoTiMA2.
Functions that do not yet implement the native route stop with a separate
workflow-specific message after the compatibility check.  Currently,
\code{ctmaFit()} and \code{ctmaInit()} are the public entry points for this
backend.  In \code{ctmaInit()}, primary studies are processed sequentially;
\code{doPar} must currently equal 1 to avoid nested R/Julia parallelism.

For \code{ctmaInit()}, the two older heterogeneity arguments have different
meanings.  \code{randomIntercepts="MANIFEST"} (or \code{"CINT"}) adds
explicit latent intercept processes to each primary-study model.  In contrast,
\code{indVarying="MANIFEST"} (or \code{"CINT"}) marks parameters as varying
between persons; with \code{backend="ctsemjulia"}, this second specification
activates native Laplace integration.  A primary-study fit records the selected
route in, for example,
\code{initNative\$studyFitList[[1]]\$ctsemjulia\_mode}.

<<ctsem-native-init-laplace, purl=FALSE>>=
initNative <- ctmaInit(
  primaryStudies = CoTiMAstudyList_3,
  n.latent = 2,
  activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
  indVarying = "MANIFEST",
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  coresToUse = -1,
  doPar = 1
)

initNative$studyFitList[[1]]$ctsemjulia_mode  # "laplace"
summary(initNative)
@

A model without random effects needs no special backend controls:

<<ctsem-native-direct, purl=FALSE>>=
nativeDirect <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  optimize = TRUE,
  coresToUse = -1,
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  backendArgs = list(
    optimcontrol = list(
      uncertainty = "hessian"
    )
  )
)

nativeDirect$ctsemjulia_mode  # "direct"
@

A pooled model may also contain person-level variation without any
study-level random effect.  For example, \code{indVarying="MANIFEST"} retains
the person-varying manifest means generated by CoTiMA2 and activates Laplace
integration.  No \code{study\_random\_effects} argument is required.  The
ordinary fixed study-indicator effects remain in the model, so this
specification does not treat observations from all primary studies as coming
from one undifferentiated study.  CoTiMA2 constructs an outer study-membership
level only when study-level random effects are requested; a subject-only model
therefore has no redundant outer grouping level or membership message.

<<ctsem-native-subject-only, purl=FALSE>>=
nativeSubjectOnly <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  indVarying = "MANIFEST",
  optimize = TRUE,
  coresToUse = -1,
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  backendArgs = list(
    optimcontrol = list(
      uncertainty = "hessian"
    )
  )
)

nativeSubjectOnly$ctsemjulia_mode  # "laplace"
@

Random effects may be selected conveniently by ctsem matrix family.  For
example, the following model gives CINT a random effect at both the subject and
study levels, while DIFFUSION and T0VAR vary between studies only:

<<ctsem-native-laplace, purl=FALSE>>=
nativeLaplace <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  optimize = TRUE,
  coresToUse = -1,
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  backendArgs = list(
    subject_random_effects = "CINT",
    study_random_effects = c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR"),
    remove_study_fixed_effects = TRUE,
    optimcontrol = list(finishsamples = 500)
  )
)

summary(nativeLaplace)
ctsem::ctLaplaceCheck(nativeLaplace)
nativeLaplace$ctsemjulia_mode  # "laplace"
@

Bayesian estimation is requested with \code{optimize=FALSE} and proper priors.
The nested \code{backendArgs\$sampleControl} list follows ctsem's native API.
\code{sampler="nuts"}, \code{"saem"}, \code{"map"}, and \code{"smc"} are supported. Common
controls are \code{chains}, \code{warmup}, \code{draws}, \code{maxDraws},
\code{processes}, and \code{seed}. NUTS also reads, among others,
\code{target\_accept}, \code{maxdepth}, \code{minESS}, and
\code{rhatTarget}. Since CoTiMA2 0.14.33, sampler, placement, chain counts,
warmup, draw budgets and process allocation default to NULL and are delegated
to ctsem. Set \code{backendArgs\$intoverpop="none"} for joint sampling or
\code{"laplace"}/\code{"augmented"} for a marginal; \code{"auto"} delegates
the target to ctsem. NUTS supports joint or marginal sampling, SAEM and map
require a joint random-effects target, and SMC requires a marginal target.
The returned mode is \code{"joint"}, \code{"laplace"}, \code{"augmented"},
or \code{"direct"}, according to the resolved route. The map sampler is NUTS
through a learned transformation, not maximum-a-posteriori optimization.

<<ctsem-native-bayes-saem, purl=FALSE>>=
nativeBayesSAEM <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  optimize = FALSE,
  priors = TRUE,
  indVarying = "CINT",
  coresToUse = 8,
  backendArgs = list(
    intoverpop = "none",
    sampleControl = list(
      sampler = "saem",
      chains = 4L,
      warmup = 1000L,
      draws = 1000L,
      maxDraws = 8000L,
      processes = TRUE,
      seed = 20261016L
    )
  )
)

nativeBayesSAEM$ctsemjulia_mode  # "joint"
@

For a Laplace-marginal NUTS fit, specify that target explicitly. This is a
different target from joint SAEM when the random-effect integral is not Gaussian:

<<ctsem-native-bayes-nuts, purl=FALSE>>=
nutsArgs <- list(
  intoverpop = "laplace",
  sampleControl = list(
    sampler = "nuts",
    chains = 4L,
    warmup = 1000L,
    draws = 1000L,
    maxDraws = 8000L,
    processes = TRUE,
    seed = 20261016L,
    target_accept = 0.9,
    maxdepth = 10L,
    minESS = 200,
    rhatTarget = 1.01
  )
)
@

\paragraph{Interpreting the native sampling controls.}
Automatic selection requires no \code{sampleControl} list. The learned-map
and SMC routes need a recent compatible ctsem revision (the adapter was
checked against source revision \code{57a9758d}, 27 October 2026); the
version number 4.0.0 alone does not identify these features.

<<ctsem-native-map-smc, purl=FALSE>>=
# Joint posterior with random effects already specified in the model:
mapArgs <- list(
  intoverpop = "none",
  sampleControl = list(sampler = "map", seed = 20261027L)
)

# Marginal posterior; SMC uses particles, not NUTS chain/warmup controls:
smcArgs <- list(
  intoverpop = "laplace",
  sampleControl = list(
    sampler = "smc", particles = 200L, coreset = TRUE,
    seed = 20261027L
  )
)
@

\code{warmup} is the number of discarded adaptation iterations per chain;
\code{draws} is the requested retained count per chain, not the total across
chains. The native sampler may extend a run to meet its effective-sample-size
and convergence targets; \code{maxDraws} limits that extension. Thus a request
for 1,000 draws with \code{maxDraws=8000} can take substantially longer than
a fixed 1,000-draw run. \code{minESS} and \code{rhatTarget} govern the native
stopping diagnostics; inspect the reported diagnostics even when the run
finishes normally. For NUTS, \code{target\_accept} controls adaptation and
\code{maxdepth} caps tree depth. These native names differ from the bundled
CoTiMA2 Julia backend's \code{max\_depth} and \code{samples} arguments.

\code{processes=TRUE} allows chains to run in separate R worker processes.
\code{coresToUse} is the total concurrent CPU budget passed to native ctsem;
it is shared by chains and their Julia threads. It does not allocate that many
cores independently to every chain. Primary studies in \code{ctmaInit()} are
still processed sequentially; parallel chains can run within each study.

The same control list works for initial primary-study fitting:

<<ctsem-native-init-bayes, purl=FALSE>>=
initBayesNative <- ctmaInit(
  primaryStudies = CoTiMAstudyList_3,
  activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
  n.latent = 2,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  optimize = FALSE,
  priors = TRUE,
  indVarying = "CINT",
  coresToUse = 8L,
  doPar = 1,
  backendArgs = list(intoverpop = "none", sampleControl = list(
    sampler = "saem",
    placement = "saem",
    chains = 4L,
    warmup = 1000L,
    draws = 1000L,
    maxDraws = 8000L,
    processes = TRUE,
    seed = 20261016L
  ))
)
@

Use \code{indVarying} only if person-level random effects are part of the
intended model; choosing a sampler does not itself require adding heterogeneity.
SAEM is particularly relevant to joint random-effects sampling. A speed
comparison should account for the different joint versus Laplace-marginal
targets, and compare effective sample size per unit time rather than raw draws.

\paragraph{Compatibility after updating ctsem.}
ctsem 4.0 integrates Laplace correction into \code{ctLaplaceCheck()}; a
separate exported \code{ctLaplaceCorrect()} is no longer required. CoTiMA2
0.14.28 and later recognize both API generations. Native Bayesian NUTS and
SAEM through CoTiMA2 require version 0.14.29 or later and a compatible ctsem
development version. Activate the isolated experimental library before loading
either package in a fresh R session. If Julia reports that precompiled
dependencies differ from those already loaded, restart R and initialize Julia
again before fitting.

Exact raw parameter labels can be supplied through
\code{subject\_parameters} and \code{study\_parameters} when only selected
cells of a matrix should vary.  The matrix masks \code{reDRIFT},
\code{reDIFFUSION}, and \code{reT0VAR} are also accepted for compatibility
with the random-effects workflow.  CoTiMA2 normally infers study membership
from its study-indicator TI predictors; unusual data structures can instead
provide \code{study\_id}, \code{study\_id\_ti}, or
\code{study\_dummy\_ti} explicitly.

The generated ctsem model retains any subject-level random effects already
requested by the CoTiMA2 specification.  The
\code{subject\_random\_effects} and \code{subject\_parameters} controls add
to them; they do not silently remove existing effects.  Conversely, a model
may contain study-level random effects without any subject-level random
effect.  This requires a ctsem development version that supports outer-level
random effects; CoTiMA2 tests for that capability and stops with an explicit
message when an older ctsem installation is detected.

This route should currently be treated as experimental.  Always inspect
optimizer convergence, run \code{ctLaplaceCheck()}, and retain the fitted
ctsem object.  Do not interpret a fit from an older branch that silently drops
an outer grouping level.

For a bounded optimisation diagnostic that deliberately omits Hessian and
posterior calculations, set
\code{backendArgs=list(optimcontrol=list(estonly=TRUE))}.  This is useful when
checking whether the outer optimiser takes effective iterations and whether
all inner random-effect modes converge.  A report of zero iterations together
with a nonzero gradient is a failed fit, not immediate convergence.  In
particular, do not calculate or interpret a Hessian when the fit reports
\code{estimate\$stalled=TRUE} or
\code{laplace\$inner\_converged=FALSE}.

The current native route estimates a full covariance among all random effects
selected at a grouping level.  The arguments \code{covariance},
\code{covarianceBlocks}, \code{reBlocks}, and \code{blockStructures} configure
the separate \code{ctmaRePrep()} workflow and are not currently passed to
\code{backend="ctsemjulia"}.  Consequently, inspect
\code{fit\$identifiability} and the random-effect-correlation section of
\code{summary(fit)}, especially when the number of studies is small relative
to the number of population SD and correlation parameters.  A marginal
random-effect SD can be estimable even when a correlation involving that
effect is not.  An independent or blockwise covariance option requires a
corresponding native ctsem API.

This is not merely a theoretical qualification.  In six independent
16-study simulations with three study-varying raw coordinates, all fits
converged accurately, but three fits flagged one weak correlation direction
in the unstructured random-effect covariance.  The marginal SDs were still
recovered reasonably on average.  Therefore, report and interpret marginal
heterogeneity separately from correlations among study effects, and treat a
weak correlation warning as a reason for parsimony or sensitivity analysis
rather than as automatic evidence that all marginal SD estimates are invalid.

\section{Moderator-associated reduction in random-effect heterogeneity}

Study-level moderators may account for part of the between-study variation in
a selected DRIFT, DIFFUSION, or T0VAR parameter.  CoTiMA2 compares this
variation with \code{ctmaReRedHet()}.  Fit one otherwise comparable model
without the focal moderators and one with them:

<<re-red-het, purl=FALSE>>=
preparedNull <- ctmaRePrep(
  model = pooledWithoutModerators,
  reDRIFT = reDRIFT,
  covariance = "independent",
  parameterization = "noncentered"
)

preparedModerated <- ctmaRePrep(
  model = pooledWithModerators,
  reDRIFT = reDRIFT,
  covariance = "independent",
  parameterization = "noncentered"
)

reNull <- ctmaReFit(
  preparedNull, estimation = "bayes", backend = "julia",
  chains = 4, backendArgs = juliaBackendArgs
)

reModerated <- ctmaReFit(
  preparedModerated, estimation = "bayes", backend = "julia",
  chains = 4, backendArgs = juliaBackendArgs
)

reduction <- ctmaReRedHet(
  unconditional = reNull,
  moderated = reModerated,
  comparison_draws = 10000,
  seed = 1234,
  practical_threshold = 0
)

summary(reduction)
@

For each random effect, the conventional descriptive pseudo-\(R^2\) is

\[
  R^2_{\mathrm{heterogeneity}}
  =
  1 -
  \frac{\tau^2_{\mathrm{moderated}}}
       {\tau^2_{\mathrm{unconditional}}}.
\]

A value of \(0.60\), for example, means that the fitted residual
between-study variance is 60\% smaller after accounting for the moderators.
A negative value is retained and means that the moderated model estimated
more residual heterogeneity.  It must not be forced to zero.

The models must use the same studies, random-effect selection,
transformations, covariance structure, and preferably the same priors.
\code{ctmaRePrep()} removes study-indicator fixed effects for hierarchical
parameters but retains substantive moderator effects.  The comparison is
performed on the raw study-effect variance scale, on which the Gaussian
hierarchical distribution is defined.

For Bayesian fits, \code{ctmaReRedHet()} independently combines draws from the
two separately fitted posterior distributions and reports an interval and the
probability that the reduction exceeds \code{practical\_threshold}.  This is a
descriptive cross-model Monte Carlo comparison, not a joint posterior
distribution and not evidence that the moderators causally explain the
heterogeneity.  With multiple random effects, the function also reports the
reduction in the sum of their marginal variances.  Changes in random-effect
correlations should be inspected separately.

The main arguments are:

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{unconditional}] The fitted \code{ctmaReFit()} model without the
    focal moderators.
  \item[\code{moderated}] The otherwise comparable fitted model containing
    the moderators.
  \item[\code{probs}] Lower, median, and upper probabilities for the
    cross-model Monte Carlo interval.
  \item[\code{comparison\_draws}] Number of independently combined
    cross-model draw pairs.
  \item[\code{seed}] Seed for reproducible pairing.
  \item[\code{practical\_threshold}] Reduction threshold used for the reported
    exceedance probability.
\end{description}

\section{Reduction in WEC-based residual heterogeneity}

\code{ctmaRedHet()} provides the corresponding two-stage comparison for
ordinary pooled \code{ctmaFit()} models fitted with \code{WEC=TRUE}.  Its
heterogeneity estimate is not the hierarchical variance component estimated
by \code{ctmaReFit()}.  Instead, it uses the reconstructed study deviations
and their standard errors:

<<red-het, purl=FALSE>>=
redHet <- ctmaRedHet(
  ctmaFitObject = pooledWithoutModerators,
  ctmaFitObjectMod = pooledWithModerators,
  dt = c(0.5, 1, 2)
)

redHet$heterogeneity_reduction
redHet$discrete_time[["Time Interval = 1"]]$reduction
@

The primary continuous-time comparison is the sampling-error-adjusted
two-stage residual \(\tau^2\).  In the moderated model, variation is measured
around each study's moderator-predicted mean.  The variance of complete study
effects around one grand mean is not used, because it would count
moderator-explained variation as remaining heterogeneity.

For every requested \code{dt}, CoTiMA2 reconstructs raw DRIFT draws through
\code{ctmaParMap()}, separately forms the complete study DRIFT and its
moderator-predicted DRIFT, and calculates

\[
  \exp(\mathbf A_{i,\mathrm{complete}}\,dt)
  -
  \exp(\mathbf A_{i,\mathrm{predicted}}\,dt)
\]

for every draw before summarising the residual study effects.  This ordering
is essential because the matrix exponential is nonlinear.  The same mapped
raw-draw representation is used for Stan and Julia fits.  Julia fits must
therefore retain transformed/raw draws; they can be added later with
\code{ctAddJuliaSamples()} when the required covariance information is stored.
Negative proportional reductions are retained and indicate an increase in
residual heterogeneity.  When the unconditional estimate is zero, the ratio
is undefined and reported as \code{NA}.  Q, \(I^2\), and the unadjusted sample
variance are secondary diagnostics.

\section{Convergence and diagnostic guidance}

\subsection{Maximum likelihood}

Do not judge convergence from the number of iterations or the visually stable
objective alone.  Inspect:

<<ml-diagnostics, purl=FALSE>>=
mlFit$optimization$converged
mlFit$optimization$maximum_absolute_gradient
mlFit$optimization$gradient_tolerance
mlFit$optimization$termination
mlFit$optimization$stage_objectives
mlFit$optimization$stage_maximum_absolute_gradients
@

An optimizer may stop below \code{maxiters} because an enabled convergence
criterion has been met.  Conversely, reaching \code{maxiters} with a large
gradient is not convergence.  When \code{carefulFit=TRUE}, iteration totals are
distributed across stages and \code{maxiters} applies to each stage.

With \code{warm\_start="auto"}, CoTiMA2 first determines whether a smaller pilot
is both useful and defensible. Complete person records are sampled within
studies; the requested fraction is enlarged when necessary for the number of
base parameters, repeated measurements, small studies, or binary categories.
For a pooled RI model, at least three observations identify an informative
person for this check. If a defensible pilot would require at least 80 percent
of all persons, the automatic pilot is skipped. After subset optimization, its
full-length candidate vector is evaluated on all data and accepted only when
its full-data objective is finite and no worse than the ordinary starting
point. Thus the pilot cannot silently replace a better default start. Details
are retained in \code{fit$warm\_start} and timing in
\code{fit$warm\_start\_timing}.

\subsubsection{Continuing an existing Julia fit}

When \code{inits} contains the complete raw estimates from an earlier fit of
the identical model, the first stage of careful fitting is often unnecessary.
Stage 1 does not reset existing TI-predictor effects to zero: it holds the
supplied TI effects fixed while re-optimizing only the population/base
parameters. Stage 2 then releases all parameters, and Stage 3 recomputes the
joint scaling and refines the solution when necessary. Starting a refinement
from an already suitable joint solution can therefore avoid Stage 1 by using
\code{carefulFit=FALSE}:

<<continue-julia-fit, eval=FALSE>>=
refineArgs <- fit_config$recommended
refineArgs$inits <- previous_fit$coefficients$raw
refineArgs$carefulFit <- FALSE
refineArgs$warm_start <- FALSE
refineArgs$optimizer_initial_step <- 0.001
refineArgs$maxiters <- 500L

refined_fit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit_O,
  backend = "julia",
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  randomIntercepts = "CINT",
  backendArgs = refineArgs
)
@

Explicit \code{inits} already disable the subset warm start, so
\code{warm\_start=FALSE} is redundant but documents the intended refinement
workflow. Reusing raw estimates is safe only when the data and their ordering,
\code{scaleTime}, binary specification, random-intercept or individual-varying
specification, TI predictors, and parameter ordering are unchanged. Otherwise
the raw vector can have a different length or meaning; staged fitting may then
remain useful, or those estimates must not be reused directly.

An almost-zero negative Hessian eigenvalue can be numerical round-off; a
clearly non-positive or ill-conditioned Hessian can indicate weak
identification, a boundary, a saddle point, or insufficient convergence.  The
backend reports curvature and regularization rather than silently presenting
an unreliable inverse.

\subsection{Bayesian NUTS}

For substantive inference, inspect at least:
\begin{itemize}
  \item retained-sample divergences (preferably zero);
  \item warmup divergences (useful evidence about adaptation geometry);
  \item maximum tree-depth hits;
  \item rank-normalized $\widehat R$ across multiple chains;
  \item bulk and tail effective sample sizes; and
  \item trace plots and substantive plausibility of posterior estimates.
\end{itemize}

Very short tests are useful only to verify that code and dimensions work.  An
$\widehat R$ or effective sample size computed from a handful of draws is not
an inferential diagnostic.  As general targets, $\widehat R$ should be close
to 1 (commonly below 1.01), effective sample sizes should be adequate for the
reported summaries, and retained divergences should be absent.  These are
diagnostic guidelines, not substitutes for model-specific judgment.

\section{Practical computing guidance}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Thread count.} The default \code{julia\_threads="auto"}
    creates a pool with one fewer thread than the CPUs available to the R
    process.  Set it before Julia is first initialized and restart R to change
    the pool capacity.  \code{subject\_threads} limits concurrent subject tasks
    and can be changed between fits without restarting R.  Keep
    \code{blas\_threads=1} unless a benchmark demonstrates a benefit.
  \item \textbf{Concurrent chains.} Budget CPU use as
    \code{parallel\_chains * threads\_per\_chain}; BLAS should normally remain at
    one thread.  Worker startup adds overhead, so parallel execution is useful
    for substantive chains rather than very short smoke tests.
  \item \textbf{ML multistart.} Multistart improves robustness and explores
    alternative basins; it does not make a single L-BFGS trajectory parallel.
    The backend waits for all starts, selects the highest-likelihood converged
    solution, and retains diagnostics for every start.  If none converges, it
    retains the best finite solution and reports nonconvergence.
  \item \textbf{Concurrent fits.} Several RStudio sessions can compete for CPU
    and memory.  Reduce Julia threads in each session so the total does not
    exceed available resources.  Heavy oversubscription can slow both jobs and
    may destabilize the R session.
  \item \textbf{Interrupting.} Use RStudio's Stop control or Escape.  An
    interrupt may be delayed until Julia returns control from the current
    likelihood or trajectory calculation.
  \item \textbf{Progress display.} The compact NUTS bar uses ETA (estimated
    time of arrival/completion).  ETA is a predicted remaining duration, not a
    mathematical time difference denoted by $\Delta$.
  \item \textbf{Save intermediate objects.} Save the unfitted setup, converged
    ML fit, and Bayesian fit separately.  This permits transformed summaries
    or random-effects preparation without repeating estimation.
\end{itemize}

<<save-results, purl=FALSE>>=
saveRDS(pooledSetup, "pooled_setup.rds")
saveRDS(mlFit, "julia_ml_fit.rds")
saveRDS(bayesFit, "julia_bayes_fit.rds")
@

\subsection[Choosing controls with ctmaFitConfig()]{Choosing controls with
\code{ctmaFitConfig()}}

For a fit expected to take hours, a short configuration pilot can compare
subject concurrency and ForwardDiff chunk sizes at the same raw starting
vector.  Model-building arguments are supplied exactly as to \code{ctmaFit()}.
The approximate time budget includes setup and warmup; a single likelihood or
gradient calculation cannot be interrupted midway.

<<fit-config, purl=FALSE>>=
fitConfig <- ctmaFitConfig(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "julia",
  time_limit = 600,
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  cint = "auto",
  binaries = c(1, 0)
)

fitConfig$benchmarks
fitConfig$optimizer_pilots
juliaBackendArgs <- fitConfig$recommended

finalFit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "julia",
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  cint = "auto",
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  backendArgs = juliaBackendArgs
)
@

The ranking criterion approximates an optimizer iteration as one gradient plus
two likelihood evaluations. Each candidate is measured repeatedly and median
times are used. Before comparing thread counts, serial and concurrent
likelihoods and gradients must agree within strict numerical tolerances. If a
concurrent component fails this check, it remains on the deterministic serial
path for the complete fit and is not presented as a parallel speed
recommendation. The check is performed once at the beginning of a fit; the
backend does not switch numerical paths during optimization.

The throughput pilot does not determine the number of iterations required for
convergence, so complete-fit runtime remains model dependent. After choosing
the best valid throughput configuration,
\code{ctmaFitConfig()} also runs short joint (\code{carefulFit=FALSE}) and
staged (\code{carefulFit=TRUE}) optimizer pilots. It recommends the alternative
using both objective improvement per elapsed second and reduction of the raw
gradient. The default is five iterations per stage; change this with, for example,
\code{pilot\_iterations=10L}. Models without time-invariant effects need no
staged optimization, so only \code{carefulFit=FALSE} is tested. These short
pilots are a heuristic and do not establish convergence.

Explicit values in \code{backendArgs} are retained as constraints. Candidate
strategies can be restricted with, for example,
\code{candidate\_carefulFit=FALSE}. With \code{backend="stan"}, the function
validates and returns the Stan controls and reports setup cost; pooled
ctsem/Stan fitting does not expose Julia's subject-task or ForwardDiff chunk
controls.

The experimental native ctsem backend can be checked in the same way:

<<ctsemjulia-fit-config, purl=FALSE>>=
nativeConfig <- ctmaFitConfig(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  cint = "auto",
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  backendArgs = list(
    optimcontrol = list(uncertainty = "hessian")
  )
)

nativeBackendArgs <- nativeConfig$recommended

nativeFit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  scaleTime = 1/12,
  cint = "auto",
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  coresToUse = 4,
  backendArgs = nativeBackendArgs
)
@

For \code{backend="ctsemjulia"}, \code{ctmaFitConfig()} validates the
unfitted setup and native backend controls and returns the resolved controls
in \code{recommended}. It deliberately does not report
\code{subject\_threads} or \code{chunksize} benchmarks: those controls and
the reusable microbenchmark interface belong to CoTiMA2's own
\code{backend="julia"}. Use the top-level \code{coresToUse} argument in the
subsequent \code{ctmaFit()} call to control worker capacity. The compatible
experimental ctsem \code{juliaFit} branch and \code{JuliaConnectoR} must be
active before running this configuration check.

\section{Portable Julia installation and first-time setup}

The most portable installation strategy uses \emph{Juliaup}, Julia's official
version manager, on Windows, macOS, and Linux.  Installation commands differ
slightly by operating system, so users should follow the current instructions
at \url{https://julialang.org/install/}.  Julia itself is not included in the
CoTiMA2 R package.  After installing or updating Julia, fully restart RStudio so
that it can inherit the changed executable search path.

For most users, the recommended one-time R command is simply:

<<automatic-julia-setup, purl=FALSE>>=
juliaSetup <- ctmaJuliaSetup(agree = TRUE)
# Restart R afterward. Fitting functions rediscover Julia automatically.
@

The remaining code in this section documents the individual discovery and
bootstrap operations performed by \code{ctmaJuliaSetup(agree = TRUE)}, and provides
manual alternatives for diagnosing an unusual installation.

Do not assume that Julia is stored in a fixed directory.  First ask the
operating system for the executable and, if necessary, check Juliaup's common
user-level locations.  The following helper also accommodates Juliaup's
versioned Linux directory, as encountered on some Ubuntu installations.

<<find-julia, purl=FALSE>>=
findJulia <- function() {
  located <- unname(Sys.which("julia"))
  if (nzchar(located)) return(normalizePath(located))

  candidates <- c(
    path.expand("~/.juliaup/bin/julia"),
    path.expand("~/.juliaup/bin/julia.exe"),
    Sys.glob(path.expand("~/.julia/juliaup/julia-*/bin/julia"))
  )
  candidates <- candidates[file.exists(candidates)]
  if (!length(candidates)) stop(
    "Julia was not found. Install Juliaup, restart RStudio, and retry."
  )
  normalizePath(tail(candidates, 1L))
}

juliaExecutable <- findJulia()
juliaHome <- dirname(juliaExecutable)

# RStudio Server may inject native-library paths that are incompatible with
# Julia. ctmaJuliaSetup(agree = TRUE) applies this Linux isolation automatically. Use the
# same wrapper only when reproducing the individual commands manually.
juliaSystem2 <- function(args, env = character()) {
  if (identical(unname(Sys.info()[["sysname"]]), "Linux")) {
    system2(
      "/usr/bin/env",
      c(
        "-u", "LD_LIBRARY_PATH", "-u", "LD_PRELOAD",
        env, shQuote(juliaExecutable), args
      )
    )
  } else {
    system2(juliaExecutable, args, env = env)
  }
}

juliaSystem2("--version")
@

JuliaCall's setup code needs the Julia packages \code{Suppressor} and
\code{RCall} in Julia's default environment, before CoTiMA2 activates its own
project.  The following one-time bootstrap keeps precompilation to one worker,
which is safer on high-core-count machines and in memory-limited sessions.

<<bootstrap-juliacall, purl=FALSE>>=
bootstrapCode <- paste(
  "import Pkg;",
  "Pkg.add([\"Suppressor\", \"RCall\"]);",
  "Pkg.build(\"RCall\")"
)
juliaSystem2(
  c("--startup-file=no", "-e", shQuote(bootstrapCode)),
  env = c(
    paste0("R_HOME=", shQuote(R.home())),
    "JULIA_NUM_PRECOMPILE_TASKS=1",
    "JULIA_PKG_PRECOMPILE_AUTO=0"
  )
)
@

Next instantiate the Julia project installed with CoTiMA2.  Instantiation and
precompilation are deliberately separated: this produces clearer errors and
avoids excessive parallel precompilation.

<<instantiate-cotima-julia, purl=FALSE>>=
juliaProject <- system.file("julia", package = "CoTiMA2")
stopifnot(nzchar(juliaProject))

instantiateCode <- paste(
  "import Pkg;",
  "Pkg.instantiate(; allow_autoprecomp=false);",
  "Pkg.status()"
)
juliaSystem2(
  c(
    "--startup-file=no",
    paste0("--project=", shQuote(juliaProject)),
    "-e", shQuote(instantiateCode)
  ),
  env = "JULIA_PKG_PRECOMPILE_AUTO=0"
)

juliaSystem2(
  c(
    "--startup-file=no",
    paste0("--project=", shQuote(juliaProject)),
    "-e", shQuote("import Pkg; Pkg.precompile()")
  ),
  env = "JULIA_NUM_PRECOMPILE_TASKS=1"
)
@

Restart R once more after this bootstrap.  \code{ctmaJuliaFit()} and
Julia-based \code{ctmaReFit()} use the same discovery routine as
\code{ctmaJuliaSetup(agree = TRUE)}, even when \code{Sys.which("julia")} remains empty.
An explicit \code{julia\_home} is therefore needed only for an unusual
installation.  A failed JuliaCall initialization also requires an R restart;
the embedded Julia runtime cannot be repaired or replaced reliably inside
that R process.
If \code{Suppressor} and \code{RCall} were already installed successfully,
\code{ctmaJuliaSetup(bootstrap\_juliacall=FALSE)} skips the default-environment
bootstrap and proceeds directly to the isolated CoTiMA2 project.  This is also
the appropriate recovery when an unrelated package such as Conda fails while
the default Julia environment is being processed.

\section{Recommended staged validation strategy}

For a new class of model:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Fit a small representative dataset through ctsem/Stan.
  \item Run \code{ctmaJuliaFit(estimation="none", validate=TRUE)} at the same
    raw estimates and require likelihood agreement within tolerance.
  \item Run a short Julia ML or NUTS smoke test to reveal dimension,
    transformation, and initialization problems.
  \item Run the intended estimation with realistic iterations and inspect all
    convergence diagnostics.
  \item Compare key transformed parameters with ctsem or known simulation
    values, not only the raw objective.
  \item Only then scale to the large dataset for which computational savings
    matter.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Troubleshooting}

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{Cannot find CtsemJulia.jl}] Supply the development path through
    \code{julia\_file}.  Installed-package users should verify that CoTiMA2 was
    installed with its \code{inst/julia} directory.
  \item[Julia thread mismatch] Restart R and make the first Julia call with the
    intended \code{julia\_threads}.  Closing only an object does not unload the
    embedded Julia runtime.
  \item[Positive-definite factorization failure] The trial parameter vector
    implied an invalid or numerically unstable covariance.  Use better
    \code{inits}, staged ML optimization, smaller initialization jitter, or
    diagnose the model parameterization.
  \item[ML stops but the raw gradient is too large] Increase \code{maxiters},
    use \code{carefulFit=TRUE}, improve \code{inits}, and inspect stage-specific
    gradients.  Merely weakening the tolerance can conceal nonconvergence.
  \item[Hessian uncertainty is skipped] First obtain raw-gradient convergence.
    If the Hessian remains non-positive, inspect identification and curvature;
    do not assume that more transformed draws solve the problem.
  \item[No transformed coefficients are stored] Use
    \code{ctAddJuliaSamples(fit, setup, ...)} and retain the returned updated
    object.
  \item[Many blank console lines] This was caused by a multi-line AdvancedHMC
    progress display passing through JuliaCall.  The backend now uses a compact
    single-line progress callback; the issue was cosmetic and did not alter
    draws.
  \item[Bayesian tree depth is always maximal] Increase \code{max\_depth} only
    after checking adaptation, metric, scaling, and warmup.  A deeper tree can
    make every iteration substantially slower.
\end{description}

\section{A compact end-to-end template}

<<complete-template, purl=FALSE>>=
# 1. Fit and inspect every primary study with CoTiMA2's Julia backend
initFit <- ctmaInit(
  primaryStudies = primaryStudies,
  activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
  n.latent = 2,
  backend = "julia",
  coresToUse = -1,
  doPar = 1
)

# 2. Optionally determine a suitable pooled-fit resource configuration
fitConfig <- ctmaFitConfig(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "julia",
  time_limit = 600
)

# 3. Fit the pooled model directly with CoTiMA2's Julia backend
mlFit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "julia",
  backendArgs = fitConfig$recommended
)

# 4. Inspect convergence, estimates, and uncertainty
print(mlFit)
summary(mlFit, digits = 4, study_specific = TRUE)
coef(mlFit, type = "study")
vcov(mlFit)
@

For a quick analysis without the configuration pilot, omit step 2 and use
\code{ctmaFit(ctmaInitFit=initFit, backend="julia")} directly.  The
low-level \code{ctmaJuliaFit()} and \code{ctAddJuliaSamples()} workflow
described earlier remains available when optimization and post-processing
must be separated deliberately.

\section{Current scope and future package integration}

The Julia source and its pinned environment are installed as CoTiMA2 package
resources.  The backend can be selected through \code{backend="julia"} in
\code{ctmaInit()} and \code{ctmaFit()}, while \code{ctmaJuliaFit()} remains available as the
explicit low-level entry point.  Backend-specific controls belong in the named
\code{backendArgs} list; this keeps Julia settings separate from the common
CoTiMA2 model specification.

The package checks the translated ctsem model before estimation and reports
branches that CtsemJulia does not yet support.  Current work should therefore
focus on automated parity tests against ctsem/Stan and on extending explicitly
reported unsupported branches, rather than silently approximating them.

\section{Ryzen native-backend validation, 27 October 2026}

CoTiMA2 0.14.33 was exercised against ctsem revision \code{57a9758d} in an
isolated Ubuntu/R library on the Ryzen, with a four-core budget. All 18 real
dispatcher tests completed: five ML fits without priors, five MAP fits with
priors, and eight sampling runs. Models covered no random effects, person
intercepts, study intercepts, both grouping levels, and a mixed continuous and
binary measurement model. The tests called the native dispatchers used by
\code{ctmaInit()} and \code{ctmaFit()}; they did not exercise an entire
user-data preparation script.

Learned-map NUTS sampled the joint posterior in the person, study-only and
combined cases. SAEM sampled the person-effects joint model; SMC sampled a
study-effects marginal model; ordinary NUTS handled the mixed binary model.
Automatic sampling selected NUTS for the tested no-effects and Gaussian
person-intercept cases. It need not select map merely because random effects
are present: Gaussian effects can be integrated exactly.

All optimization runs reported convergence, and no sampling run reported a
divergent transition. These were deliberately short smoke tests (typically
two chains, 50 warmup iterations and 62 retained draws per chain), using an
exploratory R-hat target of 1.2. Observed worst finite R-hat values were about
1.023--1.065; these do not establish publication-ready posterior convergence.
SMC has its own particle diagnostics rather than a conventional R-hat.

Two prior-free ML fits warned about unidentified directions: the study-level
intercept SD in the small study-only design and a binary-process drift in the
mixed design. Their affected SEs must not be treated as usable merely because
the optimizer converged. The fit/summary calls took approximately 4--16 seconds
after \code{ctJuliaSetup()}; installation and one-time Julia precompilation
are excluded. These timings are not a general backend or sampler speed ranking.

\section{Binary diffusion and intercept sensitivity}

Selecting a binary manifest type supplies the binary observation model; it
does not guarantee precise estimation of its latent dynamics. A small paired
Ryzen pilot (48 ML fits, ctsem revision \code{57a9758d}) found extreme free
diffusion estimates and identification warnings despite optimizer convergence.
Equivalent CINT and MANIFESTMEANS specifications could reach different local
solutions; transferring the estimates showed that both solutions existed in
both parameterizations. An intercept switch alone is therefore not a general
identification remedy.

Fix diffusion only as an explicit, defensible model or scale assumption, and
check sensitivity to its value. The pilot's fixed value was known from data
generation and cannot be inferred for an application. Inspect identification
warnings, boundary/saturation diagnostics, parameter magnitudes and multiple
starting solutions. See the Fit Diagnostics guide for the pilot's scope and
limitations; no default was changed from these results.

\noindent\textbf{Follow-up accuracy check.}
Independent state-grid and Monte Carlo integrations found materially inflated
native likelihoods for two extreme-variance solutions in this pilot (ctsem
revision \code{57a9758d}); they reversed the ranking of the paired solutions.
Fresh ctsem models reproduced the discrepancy without CoTiMA2. Thus these
extreme estimates must not be attributed solely to statistical identification.
See the Fit Diagnostics guide for numerical accuracy checks and limitations.
This result does not establish the same problem in CoTiMA2's separate
\code{backend = "julia"} implementation or in later ctsem revisions.

\section{Reading the separate fit checks}

Since CoTiMA2 0.14.34, new native Julia summaries distinguish optimizer
convergence from identification and posterior sampling diagnostics. A Bayesian
placement optimizer reaching its stopping criterion does not establish MCMC
convergence. Identification and transform-saturation flags remain visible even
when the optimizer reports convergence. Primary-study checks include study
numbers. For older fits, use \code{ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)}; missing stored
diagnostics remain unknown, and this function does not refit or start Julia.
The optional binary predictor-scale flag is explicitly heuristic and changes
neither the model nor its estimates. See the Fit Diagnostics guide for details.


\section{ctsem 4 compatibility and stored sampling diagnostics}

CoTiMA2 0.14.42 implements ctsem 4's \code{covmattransform = "z"}
in its bundled \code{backend = "julia"} covariance converter. This is a
normalised matrix exponential of symmetric off-diagonal coordinates, scaled by
marginal standard deviations; these cells must not be read as a Cholesky factor.
Older bundled-engine fits made from ctsem 4 setup with nonzero covariance cells
should be re-fitted. The native \code{backend = "ctsemjulia"} uses ctsem's own
converter and was not affected by this CoTiMA2 conversion defect. Diagonal-only
checks cannot detect this difference. Unknown future covariance codes are
rejected before starting the bundled engine.

For native Bayesian fitting, \code{iter} inside the \code{sampleControl}
list in \code{backendArgs} is now accepted and forwarded alongside ctsem's existing \code{warmup}, \code{draws}
and \code{maxDraws} controls. Their meanings and precedence remain ctsem's;
\code{iter} is not silently renamed to retained draws. Automatic sampler
selection remains delegated to ctsem. In particular, \code{sampler = "map"}
means learned-map posterior sampling, not MAP estimation. MAP estimation uses
optimisation with priors.

\code{ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)} reads stored information without calculating a
new summary or starting Julia. It handles both a native pooled model stored
directly in \code{studyFitList} and ctmaInit's list of primary fits. Placement
optimizer convergence and reported posterior convergence are separate. Raw
coordinate R-hat and the worst available retained transformed-summary R-hat
are labelled separately, with their effective sample size diagnostics retaining
the source definitions. Missing transformed diagnostics are unknown. SMC does
not have the usual between-chain R-hat; this is reported as not applicable,
with its available ESS retained. A clean report does not certify likelihood
accuracy or statistical identification.

CoTiMA2 explicitly retains \code{uncertainty = "hessian"} within
\code{optimcontrol} for native optimized fits. Current ctsem 4 defaults to \code{"checked"}; users may
request that explicitly. This compatibility repair does not change the chosen
uncertainty method automatically. Use \code{ctJuliaInstall()} to select ctsem's
supported Julia runtime rather than hard-coding an old Juliaup version path.


\section{Optimization targets and posterior uncertainty (version 0.14.43)}

The bundled \code{backend="julia"} optimizer maximizes the likelihood.
It does not implement MAP optimization. In \code{ctmaInit()}, \code{ctmaFit()}
and \code{ctmaFitConfig()}, requesting \code{optimize=TRUE, priors=TRUE}
with this backend now raises an error before fitting. Earlier versions could
silently return an ML fit for that request; such results must not be interpreted
as MAP estimates. Use \code{backend="ctsemjulia"} or \code{backend="stan"}
with \code{optimize=TRUE, priors=TRUE} for MAP. This restriction does not remove
bundled Julia Bayesian NUTS sampling with \code{optimize=FALSE}.

Uncertainty from bundled NUTS sampling is based on retained joint posterior
draws. It does not require a positive-definite likelihood Hessian at the initial
parameter vector. ML uncertainty still requires adequate optimization convergence
and valid curvature; a failed convergence or curvature check must be inspected,
not bypassed. Short pilot chains may execute successfully and still have poor
R-hat, low effective sample sizes, or divergences. Completion is not evidence of
posterior convergence.

The ctsem 4 interface also removes two historical inert Stan optimizer defaults
and renames the internal matrix registry. CoTiMA2 filters the removed defaults
only if the installed ctsem rejects them and their historical values were used;
unsupported non-default requests raise an error. Older compatible ctsem versions
retain their controls.


\section{Binary offsets and mixed mean specifications (version 0.14.44)}

The vector \code{manifestMeans=c("mmV1", 0)} freely estimates the first
manifest mean and fixes the second to zero. Scalar \code{0} fixes all manifest
means; \code{"auto"} requests automatic free labels. Explicit vectors retain
both parameter labels and numeric fixed values.

A binary measurement offset and a continuous-time process intercept have
different roles. If data are generated as a Bernoulli response with probability
\code{plogis(eta1 - 0.8)} from a centered latent process, a matching reference
uses a binary manifest offset of -0.8, centered initial latent means and zero
process intercepts. One may estimate the offset with the following specification:
\begin{verbatim}
# Relevant arguments for a two-variable binary/continuous reference model:
binaries = c(1, 0),
manifestMeans = c("mmV1", 0),
cint = 0,
T0means = 0
\end{verbatim}
This is appropriate for that generating model, not a universal recommendation
to fix all process intercepts. A free CINT changes the state mean after elapsed
time; it cannot supply a missing initial mean at time zero. Under a centered
symmetric initial Gaussian latent and a fixed symmetric logistic link with zero
manifest offset, the marginal initial binary probability is 0.5. A skewed
observed binary distribution alone does not imply any particular latent variance.

Switching from free CINTs to manifest offsets changes model constraints and
usually the induced prior distribution; it is not merely a harmless numerical
reparameterization. Check the generated model before fitting. A completed fit,
a plausible point estimate or inclusion of a true value in one simulated interval
does not establish posterior convergence or general parameter recovery.


\section{Optimizer convergence and Laplace accuracy}

For native study-level random-effects fits, optimizer convergence and the
accuracy of Laplace integration are separate questions. The optimizer can
converge to the optimum of its approximated objective while a stored comparison
with a more accurate integration method remains unresolved.

\code{ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)} and the native summary footer now report the
cached Laplace accuracy status independently. \code{ctmaTables()} includes the
same information in table notes. When available, the report includes the stored
integration gap, maximum standardized parameter shift and whether a correction
was applied. These quantities retain their upstream definitions; the total gap
is not silently divided by the number of persons or studies.

A status of \code{unresolved} is neither an accuracy pass nor proof of failure.
A status of \code{checked} without an accompanying verdict says only that an
operation was performed. Missing verdicts remain unassessed. A curvature-based
certification of optimizer convergence does not resolve these integration
questions. Diagnostic reports and table generation read stored metadata only:
they do not rerun \code{ctLaplaceCheck()}, start Julia for this metadata, change
estimates or automatically increase computational budgets.

<<laplace-status-separate, eval=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
checks <- ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit, print = FALSE)
checks$optimizer_converged
checks$laplace_accuracy
ctmaTables(fit, parameters = "drift", statistics = c("mean", "2.5", "97.5"),
           format = "none", interactive = FALSE)$notes
@


\section{Reproducible initialization and batch-safe file handling}

Automatic initialization for the Stan study-level random-effects workflow
\code{ctmaReFit()} follows the fitting \code{seed}, including an explicit
override of \code{seed} in the fitting-control list. Chain-specific initialization streams differ,
but repeated calls with the same seed and chain are reproducible. Each
initialization callback restores its caller's random-number state, including
when initialization fails. This concerns initialization; it does not promise
that an entire stochastic fitting call leaves the RNG state unchanged.

\code{ctmaOptimizeInit()} validates its required inputs before running retries.
On Unix systems parallel retries use workers owned by that call; on Windows
retries are sequential. The function does not replace a user's registered
\code{foreach} backend. In \code{ctmaGenData()}, cluster cleanup no longer
replaces restoration of the \code{scipen} option on exit.

When an output file already exists, noninteractive saving now stops with a
clear message unless \code{silentOverwrite = TRUE} was explicitly requested.
Use a new filename or directory to keep both results. Interactive saving still
offers overwrite, skip, rename and cancel; a renamed target is checked again
before an existing file can be replaced. Blank input cancels rather than
waiting indefinitely. These changes do not authorize overwriting user files
by default.

<<safe-study-re-and-saving, eval=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
# Reproducible automatic initialization for the Stan study-RE workflow:
fit <- ctmaReFit(prepared, estimation = "bayes", seed = 1234,
                 cores = 2, chains = 2)
# For scripted ctmaInit calls, explicitly choose whether saved files may change:
init <- ctmaInit(primaryStudies = studyList, n.latent = 2,
                 activeDirectory = outputDirectory, silentOverwrite = TRUE)
@


\subsection*{Native quadrature refinement and stored diagnostics}
With a compatible recent experimental ctsem, request
\code{backendArgs = list(intoverpop = "quadrature")} to refine an optimized
Laplace fit with random effects. This option requires \code{optimize = TRUE};
it is not a sampler and is not needed for a model without random effects.
The returned mode records \code{"quadrature"}. Earlier ctsem versions may
not support this explicit route.

\code{ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)} reports stored likelihood definitions separately:
\code{loglik} with its source method and reported \code{loglik_se}, and
\code{loglik_laplace}. A checked likelihood must not be substituted for the
optimized Laplace objective when comparing optimization results. Optimizer
predicted gain, resolution, convergence tolerance and convergence floor are
shown when stored. These are source-reported diagnostics, not a new common
convergence threshold. Optimizer convergence does not establish posterior
convergence or Laplace accuracy; an unresolved integration check remains
unresolved. Reading these diagnostics starts neither Julia nor a new fit.


\section*{CPU budgets and writable Julia projects (0.14.48)}
\code{coresToUse} is resolved against CPU availability reported by parallelly,
including affinity and scheduler/check restrictions. Negative values retain
available cores minus the requested reserve, with a minimum of one. Automatic
bundled Julia allocation uses this resolved budget; explicit Julia worker,
thread and BLAS combinations that exceed it fail before initialization.
The maximum of the main-process and parallel-worker phases is budgeted, because
these fitting phases run sequentially. Transformation workers are bounded too.
The native \code{ctsemjulia} backend receives the resolved \code{cores} budget;
its internal sampler allocation remains ctsem's responsibility.

Owned child processes inherit single-thread BLAS/OpenMP environment limits;
the caller's environment is restored even after errors. This does not change
the threading of an R BLAS library already loaded by the caller. External
processes and computations in other R sessions are outside this budget.

The bundled \code{julia} backend uses a content-keyed project copy under R's
CoTiMA2 user cache. Installed package files are read-only templates. Project
instantiation may update the cached manifest, while fitting does not install
missing dependencies. An explicit custom Julia source/project remains the
user's responsibility. This policy does not change ctsem's independently
managed native engine cache.

Inspect \code{ctmaJuliaSetup(dry_run = TRUE)} without creating project files
or running Julia. For setup, explicitly run
\code{ctmaJuliaSetup(agree = TRUE)}; batch sessions otherwise stop and
interactive sessions ask for consent. JuliaCall bootstrap can also update
Julia's default user environment. Restart R after bootstrap as instructed.


\section{Planned ML refits}
For pooled native \code{ctsemjulia} ML fits, \code{ctmaOptimizeFit(gambling = FALSE)}
provides bounded reference and explicitly nominated study-exclusion checks.
Its \code{quickCheck = TRUE} pilots are diagnostic only. See the User Guide and
Fit-Diagnostics guide for examples, supported models, checkpoints and the
separation of full-data optimization from subset sensitivity analyses.
The legacy randomized workflow remains the default \code{gambling = TRUE};
bundled \code{julia} refits continue to use that legacy workflow.

Automatic nominations are available with \code{referenceCandidates = "auto"}
and \code{candidateStudies = "auto"}. Inspect
\code{automaticSelection} (its \code{studyDiagnostics} component) for the original study IDs,
rankings and reasons. Nomination alone requests no study exclusions; specify
\code{exclusion} explicitly after reviewing their scientific justification.

\section{Stationary initialization and determined diffusion}
The optional arguments \texttt{stationary=FALSE} and
\texttt{determinedDiffusions=FALSE} preserve existing behavior by default.
The experimental determined-diffusion option estimates the stationary
within-person covariance; it does not fix it to the unit covariance of
standardized observed data. It currently supports ML for one or two
unrestricted processes with \texttt{stationary=TRUE}; unsupported process
constraints and study-effect mappings cause explicit errors.
For the supported combinations and model-preparation example, see
\texttt{CoTiMA2\_Stationary\_Processes\_0.14.59.Rnw}.


\section{CoTiMA2 0.14.58: retired controls and study-order safety}
The \code{experimental} and \code{activateRPB} arguments have been removed;
omit them from older calls, even when their value was \code{FALSE}.
Their former default statistical behavior is retained. Pushbullet
notifications are no longer provided.

\code{ctmaFit(primaryStudyList=...)} now preserves the exact requested
original-study order as well as membership. Initialization fits, cached
data and metadata are matched together. Raw starting values still require
the same model, study order and parameter layout. A saved six-study RI
solution was reproduced by one corrected Stan refit to within approximately
\(4\times10^{-8}\) in \(-2\ell\). Six projected weak directions remained;
this verifies the handoff, not the standard errors or confidence intervals.
Older installed packages do not acquire this correction automatically.


\section*{CoTiMA2 0.14.59: explicit summary time scales}
CoTiMA2 multiplies the original time variable by \code{scaleTime}: thus
\(t_{\mathrm{fit}}=s\,t_{\mathrm{original}}\), where \(s=\mathtt{scaleTime}\).
Restoring a CT drift or diffusion-covariance rate to the original units
multiplies its estimate, SE/SD and interval limits by \(s\). For example,
a fitted drift of \(-1.2\) with \(s=1/12\) corresponds to \(-0.1\) in the
original units. This operation does not multiply Time-0/state variances,
raw optimizer coordinates, likelihood, z-values, ESS or R-hat.

Conventional initialization and pooled summaries retain their existing
fields and add \code{estimates_fitted_time_scale},
\code{estimates_original_time_scale}, and \code{time_scale_info}.
For initialization, the common original-scale alias is the drift table;
the existing original-scale diffusion and CI fields remain available.
For native pooled summaries the appended original-scale rate table contains
DRIFT and DIFFUSIONcov, not a raw diffusion-factor matrix or arbitrary
auxiliary coordinates. Other native summary sections retain their existing
definitions. Returned summary precision is retained; no model is re-fitted.

Native ctsem discrete-time intervals still refer to fitted time units.
Their original-unit equivalents are recorded in \code{time_scale_info}:
\(\Delta t_{\mathrm{original}}=\Delta t_{\mathrm{fit}}/s\). DT coefficients
are not multiplied by \(s\). To request a native DT effect for an original
lag \(h\), pass \code{timeinterval=h*scaleTime} to the pooled native summary.
This reporting update is included in CoTiMA2 0.14.59; old fits can be summarized after updating without refitting.

\end{document}
